WebSocketServer.getPort() returns the port of it's InetSocketAddress, which is 'wrong' when the socket address has a port value of 0.  From the InetSocketAddress javadoc: "A valid port value is between 0 and 65535. A port number of zero will let the system pick up an ephemeral port in a bind operation."  (http://docs.oracle.com/javase/6/docs/api/java/net/InetSocketAddress.html).  

What this all boils down to is that if a WebSocketServers' _address_ has a port of 0, we need to reference the ServerSocketChannel object to discover which port the server is really listening on.  Something like the following code should work:

``` java
public int getPort() {
        int port = getAddress().getPort();
        if( port == 0 && server != null ) {
            port = server.socket().getLocalPort();
        }
        return port;
}
```

WebSocketClient's interruptableRun() doesn't close it's selector if the connection is refused.  The connection refused appears as a java.net.ConnectException, which is a subclass of IOException.  Looking at the code below you can see that 'conn' is closed if an IOException is caught, but there's a return statement which bypasses the code where selector.close() is called.

```
    } catch ( IOException e ) {
        onError( e );
        conn.close( CloseFrame.ABNROMAL_CLOSE );
        return;
    } catch ( RuntimeException e ) {
        // this catch case covers internal errors only and indicates a bug in this websocket implementation
        onError( e );
        conn.closeConnection( CloseFrame.BUGGYCLOSE, e.toString(), false );
        return;
    }

    try {
        selector.close();
    } catch ( IOException e ) {
        onError( e );
    }
    closelock.lock();
    selector = null;
    closelock.unlock();
    try {
        client.close();
    } catch ( IOException e ) {
        onError( e );
    }
    client = null;
```

This will leave open file handles, which eventually will bring down a machine.  

