    at org.java_websocket.WebSocketClient.interruptableRun(WebSocketClient.java:198)
    at org.java_websocket.WebSocketClient.run(WebSocketClient.java:153)
    at java.lang.Thread.run(Thread.java:680)

Thanks.

The conn instance is created with this procedure:
1. client.connect();
2. client starts a thread
3. in the interruptableRun() method, call the factory to create a conn instance.

If the CPU is a bit slow and it happens you run some big chunk of computation on an android phone, this might take a while. If you call the connect() method and then immediately call getReadyState(), the conn sometimes could be null hence a NPE is thrown.

We should change the existing method a little bit to avoid this:

Existing code:

<pre>
    public int getReadyState() {
        return conn.getReadyState();
    }
</pre>


Should be 

<pre>
    public int getReadyState() {
                if (conn == null) return WebSocket.READY_STATE_CONNECTING;
        return conn.getReadyState();
    }
</pre>


