Communication via wss in general works fine by now.

Unfortunately closing  wss connections is implemented in a very basic way:

``` java
public void close() throws IOException {
        sslEngine.closeOutbound();
        sslEngine.getSession().invalidate();
        if( sc.isOpen() )
            sc.write( wrap( emptybuffer ) );
        sc.close();
    }
```

It is possible that _sc.write_ will not send enough bytes ( i measured 37 bytes ) at once to complete the TLS close notify. Calling _sc.write_ in a loop might block the current thread indefinitely so that is not an option.

**As a result your peer host might complain about an unexpectedly closed connection after _HE_ initiated the websocket close handshake.**

It will take me a few days to implement the close handshaking the nonblocking way.

Please excuse my humble English.

comments.body:
Usually i would say:

``` java
@Override
public ServerHandshakeBuilder onWebsocketHandshakeReceivedAsServer( WebSocket conn, Draft draft, ClientHandshake request ) throws InvalidDataException {
    Set<WebSocket> con = this.connections();
    synchronized ( con ) {
        if( con.isEmpty() )
            return super.onWebsocketHandshakeReceivedAsServer( conn, draft, request );
    }
    throw new InvalidDataException( CloseFrame.POLICY_VALIDATION, "maximum number of clients excedeed" );
}
```

This approach establishes the tcp connection and lets you decide if you want accept a connection based on the handshake data. E.g. someone could require a certain cookie to be set...

But since that is not exactly what you want to do i just made a new commit. Therefore you should pull before you try this approach: 

``` java
@Override
protected boolean onConnect( SelectionKey key ) {
    Set<WebSocket> con = connections();
    synchronized ( con ) {
        if( con.isEmpty() )
            return true;
    }
    return false;
}
```

This way the new connection will be refused as soon as even possible!

Unfortunately _connections();_ returns only fully connected websocket connections. Therefore the server will accept multiple connections if they connect simultaneously. :(

To work around this racing condition i would additionally use:

``` java
@Override
public void onOpen( WebSocket conn, ClientHandshake handshake ) {
    Set<WebSocket> con = this.connections();
    synchronized ( con ) {
        if( con.size() > 1 )
            conn.close( CloseFrame.POLICY_VALIDATION );
    }
}
```

If its no problem for you that you refuse connections only after the opening handshake has been performed you could just stick to the last snippet and ignore the other ones.<br>
When you refuse connections via snippet 1 and 2 the remote host will never know why you refused in order to transfer close code and reason the connection would have to be established first. So if you think that a connecting client should be informed why you refuse the connection you should also stick to the last approach.

