Android gives you some lifecycle methods but for example, there is no guarantee that it calls its onDestroy method. 
@richdyang So what you would have to do ist start/stop the WebSocket server onPause/onResume of your App/[Activity Livecylce](https://developer.android.com/guide/components/activities/activity-lifecycle.html) so that it would be removed from the system if the user kills the app. So this is a bit stupid because you couldn't do large file-transfers or keep the WebSocket connection alive. To keep it alive you would have to write an Android Service for your App. How to handle Services is described here [Processes and Application Lifecycle](https://developer.android.com/guide/components/activities/process-lifecycle.html) 

