This library doesn't know anything about "rooms", so you will need to store that information in your own data structures. For example, you could have a `Map<Integer, Collection<WebSocket>>` and also store the room id of each WebSocket using `webSocket.setAttachment()`. 

Now you can get the room of each WebSocket using `webSocket.getAttachment()` and broadcast a message to all clients of a room using `broadcast("Hello", map.get(roomId))`.

You can't listen for events from a specific room, but you can get the room of the websocket and then decide what to do with it.

Example code:
```java
Map<Integer, Collection<WebSocket>> map = new HashMap<>();

@Override
public void onMessage(WebSocket websocket, String message) {
    int roomId = <extract room ID using handshake.getResourceDescriptor()>
    // store the room id in websocket
    websocket.setAttachment(roomId);
    // update room map
    if(!map.containsKey(roomId))
        map.put(roomId, new ArrayList());
    map.get(roomId).add(websocket);
}

@Override
public void onMessage(WebSocket websocket, String message) {
    int roomId = websocket.getAttachment();
    // broadcast the message to all clients of this room
   broadcast(message, map.get(roomId));
}

@Override
public void onClose(WebSocket websocket, ...) {
    int roomId = websocket.getAttachment();
    // remove client from map
   map.get(roomId).remove(websocket);
}
```

