Is there a different way to catch the message which isn't event based, something like:

```
ExampleClient c = new ExampleClient(new URI( "ws://localhost:8887"));
c.connect();
c.send("Hello, World!");
String response = c.getResponse();
```

This is the simplest way to do this:
In your event handler, push to a BlockingQueue:
```java
BlockingQueue<String> messageQueue = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(100);
@Override
public void onMessage(String message) {
    messageQueue.put(message);
}
```
Then you can use all the facilities of blocking queue when processing messages - wait for value, wait with timeout, etc:
```java
ExampleClient c = new ExampleClient(new URI( "ws://localhost:8887"));
c.connectBlocking();
c.send("Hello, World!");
String response = c.messageQueue.take();
```
If the messages arrive faster than you can process, the `put()` call in `onMessage()` will block which is what would happen normally.

Keep in mind that there are also binary messages which are separate from text ones, so if you want to process those, you would either need to use two queues or change the type to BlockingQueue<Object> and put both kinds in the same queue.

